ll managed nodes (node1, node2)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For node1: pre-restart boot_id (bdd5de93...) and health captured, reboot acknowledged (stream closed as expected), connection reset observed during the transition, post-restart boot_id changed (eee6b2ad...), and opsbot account/keys/outbound SSH to node2 reverified working. For node2: pre-restart boot_id (dfeb3b70...), reboot ack, connection reset, post-restart boot_id changed (dc9b465e...), and opsbot account/authorized_keys plus inbound SSH from node1 reverified working. Fixed lifecycle snapshots independently confirm both boot_id transitions (before_prepare bdd5de93->after_executor eee6b2ad for node1; before_prepare dfeb3b70->after_executor dc9b465e for node2), and show no failed services or package-health issues post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6f1df013f47d432d\",\"cmd-59690026bee54174\",\"cmd-97caff3479c94ab7\",\"cmd-6b96fba6aac54368\",\"cmd-6fcab2e3d9234a70\",\"cmd-338169ae1f294eda\",\"cmd-08b2be09a84e4b4e\",\"cmd-451287adebb54f8a\",\"cmd-432f4bd0811f4725\",\"cmd-b844ffc6e22b450d\",\"cmd-b214c2cf708044b8\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\"]}],\"confidence\":0.8,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"The only state-changing commands beyond the core account/key/sshd-config work were ssh-keygen -A (generating missing host keys) and mkdir /run/sshd — both were necessary prerequisites discovered while validating sshd config with 'sshd -t' (host keys and the privilege-separation directory were absent on this image), not troubleshooting experiments unrelated to the task. systemctl restart ssh.socket was needed to activate the socket-activated sshd with the new host keys and config. One diagnostic SSH attempt from opsbot@node1 to itself (cmd-852039286ba64aff) failed with Permission denied but was read-only and caused no state change. Fixed lifecycle snapshots show only expected boot-id-tied /tmp and socket churn from the reboots, no residual failed services, and consistent dpkg package health before and after. No unnecessary or unreverted mutations were identified.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9e8e25d4347d4877\",\"cmd-9d52e7e200164a47\",\"cmd-c43bed928fe64c81\",\"cmd-98136a6537c64c1f\",\"cmd-852039286ba64aff\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The opsbot account was created on both nodes and node1's opsbot can authenticate to and run commands on node2's opsbot via ed25519 SSH key, verified both before and after a full restart of each node with matching boot_id transitions. Key-only enforcement (Match User opsbot with AuthenticationMethods publickey) was deployed, syntax-validated, and confirmed to persist across restarts on both nodes, and a rejected-password-login was directly demonstrated against node2's opsbot account both before and after its restart. The one gap is that no command in the trace performs an actual password-login attempt against node1's own opsbot account — enforcement there is supported by identical configuration and passing 'sshd -t', but not by a directly observed rejected login, so that specific outcome is only partially confirmed. Operational hygiene is good: the only mutations beyond the core task (host-key generation, /run/sshd creation, socket restart) were necessary prerequisites for the sshd config to take effect, with no unreverted or unrelated changes.\",\"limitations\":[\"No command in the captured trace attempts a password-only login directly against node1's opsbot account; the rejection is inferred from identical Match-block configuration and passing sshd -t rather than an observed failed 